1740: there is no stabilizer. This proves the toric normal-bundle
1741: description used below for interior components.
1742: 
1743: ## 25. Compact interior normal-link characteristic residues
1744: 
1745: This section computes a precisely oriented local quantity.
1746: Let \(F\) be one compact fixed component in the smooth
1747: complex fourfold atlas, with its complex orientation.
1748: Let its complex normal roots be \(x_i\), and retain the
1749: nonzero physical circle weights \(w_i\). Orient its normal
1750: sphere boundary by the original complex normal orientation,
1751: then use the positive physical orbit to orient the quotient.
1752: With \(u\) the corresponding degree-two circle variable,
1753: the rational characteristic residue of that normal link is
1754: \[
1755:  {\cal R}(F)=
1756:  [u^{-1}]\int_F
1757:  \frac{4u^3-u\{p_1(TF)+\sum_i(x_i+w_i u)^2\}}
1758:       {24\prod_i(x_i+w_i u)}.                              \tag{SZ.57}
1759:  \]
1760: The coefficient is taken in the Laurent expansion at infinity.
1761: This formula retains finite circle stabilizers: its integral
1762: is the rational orbifold pushforward, rather than an
1763: unjustified smooth replacement of a weighted projective link.
1764: 
1765: Here is the actual bundle calculation. On the sphere bundle,
1766: the tangent bundle together with the radial real line is
1767: the pullback of \(TF\) and its complex normal bundle.
1768: Quotienting the orbit removes the physical orbit line,
1769: so the quotient tangent bundle has the same stable
1770: Pontryagin class as the equivariant restriction of
1771: \(TF\oplus\nu_F\). The equivariant Thom class restricts to
1772: \(\prod_i(x_i+w_i u)\). Integrating on the normal sphere
1773: quotient is therefore the coefficient of \(u^{-1}\) after
1774: division by this Euler class. This can also be obtained by
1775: applying the localized pushforward identity to the normal
1776: disc and its sphere boundary. The exact primary integration
1777: identity is Atiyah--Bott, *The moment map and equivariant
1778: cohomology*, Topology 23 (1984), printed page 9, formula
1779: (3.8), with normal Euler formula (3.7) and localization
1780: Theorem (3.5).
1781: 
1782: The residue normalization has a direct fibre check. Conjugate
1783: each negative-weight normal line, keeping the physical
1784: circle orientation. For positive weights the fibre is the
1785: weighted projective quotient and the circle class \(u\)
1786: is the positive hyperplane class. Its projective-bundle
1787: relation is \(\prod_i(x_i+w_i u)=0\), and its fibre
1788: integral of \(u^{r-1}\) is \(1/\prod_iw_i\). The latter
1789: follows from the actual power map
1790: \([z_i]\mapsto[z_i^{w_i}]\), whose generic degree is
1791: \(\prod_iw_i\) and whose pullback of \(u\) is the
1792: ordinary hyperplane class. The relation then determines
1793: all higher pushforwards recursively, exactly as the
1794: coefficient of \(u^{-1}\) in the reciprocal Euler
1795: polynomial. A conjugated negative line changes
1796: \((x_i,w_i)\) to \((-x_i,-w_i)\) and reverses the
1797: original normal orientation. Its denominator sign in
1798: (SZ.57) is precisely that reversal. This proves both
1799: the normalization and the signed-weight formula.
1800: 
1801: For a point (SZ.57) is
1802: \[
1803:        \frac{4-\sum_i w_i^2}{24\prod_i w_i}.
1804:  \]
1805: For a curve it is
1806: \[
1807:  -\frac1{24\prod_iw_i}
1808:   \int_F\sum_i\left\{\frac{4-\sum_jw_j^2}{w_i}
1809:                                  +2w_i\right\}x_i.        \tag{SZ.58}
1810:  \]
1811: For a surface put
1812: \[
1813:  P=w_1w_2,\quad c=4-w_1^2-w_2^2,\quad
1814:  A=\frac{x_1}{w_1}+\frac{x_2}{w_2},\quad
1815:  B=\frac{x_1^2}{w_1^2}
1816:        +\frac{x_1x_2}{w_1w_2}+\frac{x_2^2}{w_2^2}.
1817:  \]
1818: Then it is
1819: \[
1820:  \int_F\frac{cB+2(w_1x_1+w_2x_2)A
1821:                     -p_1(TF)-x_1^2-x_2^2}{24P}.           \tag{SZ.59}
1822:  \]
1823: For example weights \((1,-1)\) give
1824: \((p_1(TF)-3(x_1-x_2)^2)/24\). Conjugating the
1825: negative-weight normal line reverses the normal orientation
1826: and changes this to the positive-Hopf expression. That
1827: orientation reversal is retained; neither expression has
1828: yet been identified with the completed family's Jin
1829: boundary convention.
1830: 
1831: For an interior component choose an auxiliary cocharacter
1832: \(\epsilon\) in its canonical unwrapped lattice. If a chart
1833: occurs at period \(n\), its auxiliary tangent and normal
1834: weights are exactly
1835: \[
1836:                   C^{-1}T_n\epsilon.                       \tag{SZ.60}
1837:  \]
1838: This is the dual of the literal map
1839: \(C_{\rm unwrapped}=T_{-n}C\). The compact-component
1840: pushforward (3.8) divides (SZ.58) or (SZ.59) at each
1841: auxiliary fixed point by the product of its tangent
1842: weights. All coefficients are rational numbers computed
1843: from integer matrices.
1844: 
1845: The certificate
1846: \(\texttt{source\_zero\_interior\_residues.py}\) uses two
1847: independent generic auxiliary circles for every component.
1848: It verifies equality component by component, and exports
1849: every value, rather than using only agreement of totals.
1850: The sums over the compact interior components are
1851: \[
1852:  \begin{array}{c|r|r}
1853:  (a,b,N)&\text{components}&\sum{\cal R}(F)\\ \hline
1854:  (2,1,6)&4420&1299\\
1855:  (1,2,8)&21776&4893/8.
1856:  \end{array}                                                \tag{SZ.61}
1857:  \]
1858: These sums do not include the components tagged H, E or I.
1859: 
1860: ## 26. Exact H-base normal degrees, including all three marked points
1861: 
1862: Put \(w=q_2/q_1\) on the exceptional base curve H.
1863: Its marked points are \(w=0,1,\infty\), belonging to W,
1864: E and I respectively. Use \(q_1\) as a generic normal
1865: coordinate to H. The exact periods (SZ.44) have normal
1866: orders given by H, and their remaining nonconstant
1867: factors on this base curve are
1868: \[
1869:  \begin{split}
1870:  h:\ &(Z,V)\longmapsto
1871:            (wZ,\ w^{-a}V),\\
1872:  g:\ &(Z,V)\longmapsto
1873:            (w^b Z,\ w^{-2}(1-w)^N V),
1874:  \end{split}                                                \tag{SZ.62}
1875:  \]
1876: after the powers of \(q_1\) have been written separately.
1877: All omitted factors here are the explicitly retained
1878: nonzero constants \(u_Q(0),u_P(0),u_X(0),u_v(0)\),
1879: and \(2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0\). In particular the
1880: \((1-w)^N\) term is not removed.
1881: 
1882: Consider any regular rank-three face on H. Its three
1883: primitive vectors, after dropping the zero first base
1884: coordinate, form a unimodular \(3\)-by-\(3\) matrix
1885: \(B_H\). Its orbit closure is a curve with the actual
1886: base parameter \(w\), and hence is \(\mathbb P^1\).
1887: For its \(j\)-th normal line use the generic meromorphic
1888: coordinate
1889: \[
1890:               \eta_j=Z^{p_j}V^{q_j}q_1^{s_j},
1891:        \qquad (p_j,q_j,s_j)=\text{row}_j(B_H^{-1}).          \tag{SZ.63}
1892:  \]
1893: The actual occurrence of this face in a sector chart has
1894: a recorded period \(n=(n_1,n_2)\). Let
1895: \((p,q,r,s)\) be the dual row of that chart belonging,
1896: under the recorded permutation, to this normal ray.
1897: The exact lattice checks are
1898: \[
1899:  (p,q)=(p_j,q_j),\qquad
1900:        s+(p,q)Hn=s_j.                                      \tag{SZ.64}
1901:  \]
1902: Consequently its coordinate pulls back to \(\eta_j\)
1903: times a rational unit on the base. Write
1904: \[
1905:  e_w=p(n_1+bn_2)+q(-an_1-2n_2),\qquad e_1=Nn_2q.
1906:  \]
1907: The additional base factors of the three sector charts
1908: are literal:
1909: \[
1910:  \begin{array}{c|cc|c}
1911:  \text{sector}&x&y&
1912:        \text{order of the coefficient at its marked point}\\ \hline
1913:  W&w&q_1&e_w+r\\
1914:  E&(1-w)/u_v&q_1&e_1+r\\
1915:  I&w^{-1}&wq_1&-e_w+r-s-e_1 .
1916:  \end{array}                                                \tag{SZ.65}
1917:  \]
1918: The last row uses the order at infinity. For each normal
1919: line, the sum of these three orders is its degree.
1920: Indeed the generic normal vector with \(\eta_j\)
1921: coefficient one has exactly these coefficients in the
1922: three holomorphic normal frames, so their zero and pole
1923: orders give its divisor. This fixes the line-bundle
1924: sign without selecting a different normal convention.
1925: 
1926: The fibre circle with cocharacter \((1,K,0,0)\) commutes
1927: with every exact period and all the units in (SZ.62).
1928: It therefore acts on every compact H fixed component
1929: and on its actual normal bundle. On an H fixed surface
1930: its fixed set consists of the rank-three H curves just
1931: described, together with isolated points in the
1932: intervening regular charts. There are no omitted fixed
1933: curves in a sector interior: a three-dimensional face
1934: whose base image has rank two has only a one-dimensional
1935: fibre kernel, already spanned by the physical delta;
1936: the chosen independent fibre circle is not in that span.
1937: 
1938: For an auxiliary fixed curve in such a surface, let
1939: \(\eta\) be its auxiliary normal weight, \(d_t\) the
1940: degree of that normal line, \(\beta_i,d_i\) the
1941: auxiliary weights and degrees of the two ambient
1942: normal lines. Let \(R\) denote the polynomial in
1943: (SZ.59). Substitute
1944: \[
1945:  x_i=\beta_i u+d_i h,\quad
1946:  p_1(TF)=\eta^2u^2+2\eta d_t hu,\quad h^2=0.
1947:  \]
1948: Writing the result as \(R_0u^2+R_hhu\), the exact
1949: curve contribution is
1950: \[
1951:                          R_h/\eta-R_0d_t/\eta^2.            \tag{SZ.66}
1952:  \]
1953: This is division by the actual auxiliary Euler class
1954: \(\eta u+d_t h\). The tangent Pontryagin class of
1955: the base \(\mathbb P^1\) is zero. Isolated points
1956: use the two nonzero tangent weights in (3.8).
1957: 
1958: As independent arithmetic checks, this same localization
1959: computes \(c_1(TF)^2,c_2(TF)\) for every H surface.
1960: Each auxiliary point contributes
1961: \((\beta_1+\beta_2)^2/(\beta_1\beta_2)\) and \(1\);
1962: each auxiliary fixed curve contributes \(d_t+4\)
1963: and \(2\). The resulting signature
1964: \((c_1^2-2c_2)/3\) and holomorphic Euler number
1965: \((c_1^2+c_2)/12\) are integral. Two independent
1966: fibre circles give the same value for every surface
1967: and for every normal-link residue.
1968: 
1969: The executed certificate
1970: \(\texttt{code/part3\_fourfold/source\_zero\_h\_residues.py}\)
1971: retains all three point orders, periods, permutations,
1972: normal degrees, auxiliary weights and characteristic
1973: values. Its exact totals are
1974: \[
1975:  \begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
1976:  (a,b,N)&\text{H components}&\text{full-fibre fixed curves}
1977:                 &\sum_H{\cal R}(F)\\ \hline
1978:  (2,1,6)&28&52&-5171/4\\
1979:  (1,2,8)&383&516&-4873/8.
1980:  \end{array}                                                \tag{SZ.67}
1981:  \]
1982: Thus the combined compact interior and H contributions
1983: are \(25/4\) and \(5/2\), respectively. They are local
1984: normal-link characteristic sums. The I and E continuations,
1985: their cap chain-index identifications, the global oriented
1986: boundary map, and the source-family return are not
1987: assigned by this calculation.
1988: 
